     1,
     "<div><div style=\"font-family:times new roman,new york,times,serif;font-size:12pt;color:#000000\">  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Dear all,</span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> </span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">I recently presented a poster at the IUSSI conference in Copenhagen where I had to paste in ggplot2 graphs saved as PDFs. (I think I’ve been the first social insect researcher using ggplot2. :-) On my Windows operated laptop I was unable to do so but a friend of mine managed to paste in the PDFs for me on his Mac without losing any resolution so that I could proceed in finishing the poster on my laptop. Unfortunately, I’m a perfectionist but not yet good in R. (Not a good combination, I know.) Thus, is it possible in Windows to transfer a ggplot2 graph as a vector graph (e.g. PDF) into PowerPoint or Word so that it remains crystal clear? I’m\n sorry to bother you with a question that is only related to ggplot2 but I already asked several people and no-one had a solution for Windows.</span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> </span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Many thanks,</span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\"> </span></p>  <p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-bottom:0.0001pt;line-height:normal\"><span lang=\"EN-GB\">Martin</span></p>  <br><div> </div><p style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\"><font face=\"arial, helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><strong>Martin H. Kärcher</strong></font></p><p style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\" class=\"MsoNormal\"><font face=\"arial, helvetica, sans-serif\" size=\"2\"><strong></strong>Laboratory of Apiculture &amp; Social\n 